2. WHAT THE APP IS
Octant is a GNSS instrument. It shows what your iPhone's own receiver and
sensors report about where you are — coordinates in several formats, the
accuracy of the fix, three altitude references, heading, level, speed and course
— and it computes where the navigation satellites are above you from published
orbital data. It is not a map, it holds no map data, and it operates no hosted
service. Everything it shows is produced on your device.

3. ACCURACY, AND WHAT THE APP CANNOT KNOW
The App reports the position, accuracy figure and timestamp that iOS provides,
and nothing beyond that.
- iOS exposes no per-satellite signal levels, no list of satellites actually
  being tracked, and no raw measurements to any third-party app. The satellites
  shown in the sky view are therefore computed positions, labelled as such, not
  a reading of what your receiver hears.
- Accuracy is an estimate produced by the operating system. Sky obstruction,
  buildings, weather and interference all affect a real fix.
- The three altitudes disagree by design: height above mean sea level, height
  above the WGS-84 ellipsoid, and a barometric value that depends on air
  pressure and on how recently it was calibrated.
- Compass heading depends on the magnetometer and is affected by nearby metal,
  magnets and cases.
The App is an instrument for observation and record-keeping. It is not certified
for surveying, aviation, marine navigation, or any other safety-of-life use, and
must not be relied upon as a sole means of navigation where an incorrect
position could cause injury, loss, or damage. Carry an independent means of
navigation when the consequences warrant one.

